{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch7JyuFczDS1rT6RRbUTN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Before I draft, a few questions. The first one matters most.\n\n1. **Can the email say \"your own regular cleaner, plus a named backup you'll know ahead of time\" instead of \"the same cleaner every single time\"?** Your new system has a backup, so sometimes someone else will come. Seven of 18 cleaners left last year, so people will also leave again. If the email promises \"every single time,\" the first sick day or resignation breaks that promise. And these customers already left over this exact problem. The regular-plus-backup version is true. It also answers their complaint directly: no more strangers at the door.\n2. **How do customers claim the $99 first cleaning?** For example: reply to the email, call a number, or book through a link. Does the offer have a deadline?\n3. **After the $99 visit, does the price go back to $165?** I'd rather say so plainly than let it come as a surprise. Saying it up front also lets the email explain the $25 gap compared with the apps (employees, $21/hour, insured every visit).\n4. **Who signs it, and what's the company name?** An email from you as the owner usually works better for win-backs than one from \"The Team.\"\n5. **Can the email mention the cleaner's name or the customer's old cleaner?** If your system can insert something like \"Maria is still with us,\" that's the strongest line you could have. If not, I'll write it without one."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":396,"cache_read_input_tokens":9536,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":396},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":nu